 5. 🍳 AI is cookin&#x27; 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Tiffany Herring/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Americans are deeply skeptical of virtually everything to do with AI — except when it comes to making dinner, Axios' Ben Berkowitz writes. 

 Why it matters: The AI industry has a giant problem with public perception that's only getting worse. 🍝 By the numbers: An NBC News poll of 7,105 American adults, out yesterday, asked what kinds of information they'd be comfortable getting from AI.

 74% said they'd trust AI for recipes, the only category where a majority were confident in what chatbots had to say. ⚠️ Stunning stat: Nothing else broke 34%, and some categories (like parenting advice or where to vote) didn't even top 20%.

 🥣 Between the lines: AI actually has a pretty strong track record behind the stove, despite all the controversy over its other uses.

 A Utah State University study last summer found people were equally willing to take cooking advice from AI as from nutrition students. The intrigue: Aside from the recipe result, the rest of the NBC poll was pretty grim for AI — 73% said they were worried about misinformation, and 81% said the government wasn't doing enough to regulate the technology.

 Explore the data . 🦾 Tweet du jour Screenshot: @JensenHuang /X Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang tweeted yesterday about OpenAI's powerful new GPT-6 Astra model, unveiled Thursday: "AGI has arrived" — referring to artificial general intelligence, defined as human-level capability.

 
 
