 7. 🇩🇪 Far right&#x27;s German jolt 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Ulrich Siegmund, lead candidate of the Alternative for Germany (AfD) party, and his wife, Julia Siegmund, arrive to vote yesterday in Tangermünde, eastern Germany. Photo: Annegret Hilse/Reuters Germany's far-right Alternative for Germany (AfD) won a record 44% in the eastern state of Saxony-Anhalt yesterday, bringing the party closer than ever to forming Germany's first far-right state government since World War II, Axios' Andrew Pantazi writes . 

 The AfD fell just short of a majority. The election was only regional but has major national implications. German Chancellor Friedrich Merz had vowed to weaken the AfD. Instead, his party was crushed yesterday. The AfD agenda includes heavy restrictions on immigration, restoring ties with Russia, cutting support to Ukraine and quitting the euro. (Reuters) 

