 8. 🍿 1 film thing: Hollywood&#x27;s banner summer 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Matt Damon as Odysseus in "The Odyssey." Photo: Melinda Sue Gordon/Universal Pictures via AP It's a wrap on Hollywood's best summer in a decade.

 After years of new-normal ticket sales and regular spells of unease, the summer box office rallied to $4.61 billion in domestic ticket sales between May 1 and Aug. 31, according to Rentrak. 🎟️ That's a 26.1% increase from 2025 and better than every summer other than 2013. Accounting for inflation and higher ticket prices, it's less of a record season, but 2026 was still the summer Hollywood got its mojo back. ( AP ) 

 Inde Navarrette and Michael Johnston in "Obsession." Photo: Focus Features via AP Gen-Z audiences filled theaters and turned low-budget films, including "Obsession," into blockbusters.

 Global ticket sales are up 15% compared with last year. Five movies have passed $1 billion in ticket sales, Bloomberg reports ( gift link ). 

 "Spider-Man: Brand New Day," the biggest movie of the summer, has grossed $2.4 billion worldwide. No. 2 — "The Odyssey" — has made $1.63 billion. 🥊 Reality check: Higher ticket prices "disguise a deeper decline in attendance," the N.Y. Times notes . "From Jan. 1 through Aug. 16, domestic theaters sold an estimated 568.4 million tickets, down 30% from the same period in 2019, according to S&amp;P Global Market Intelligence. That amounts to roughly 248 million missing admissions." ( Gift link ) 

 
 
